.TH "borders" "1" "2025-09-18"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
JankyBorders - A window border system for macOS
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBborders [options]\fR
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBJankyBorders\fR is a lightweight tool designed to add colored borders to
user windows on macOS 14.\&0+.\& It enhances the user experience by visually
highlighting the currently focused window without relying on the accessibility
API, thereby being faster than comparable tools.\&
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fBstyle=<style>\fR
.RS 4
Specifies the style of the border (either \fIround\fR or \fIsquare\fR)
For adaptive round borders, windows reporting a corner radius of \fI0\fR or \fI1\fR
remain completely square across both double-border layers.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBanimation=<modes>\fR
.RS 4
Specifies one or more animation modes for the focused window
border, separated by commas.\& Available modes: \fInone\fR (no
animation), \fIfade\fR (opacity fades in), \fIramp\fR (glow grows
from zero to full), \fIslide\fR (border slides from old window
position), \fIpulse\fR (adaptive breathing effect that expands thin borders
more than thick borders).\& Multiple modes can be combined, e.\&g.\&
animation=fade,ramp or animation=slide,pulse.\& Selected modes
run simultaneously.\& The old border
immediately switches to its inactive color and only the newly focused
border animates.\& Focus changes while the primary mouse button is held
are applied without animation.\& Ramp requires a glow color.\& Use
animation=none by itself to disable animations.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBanimation_duration=<seconds>\fR
.RS 4
Sets the duration shared by all selected focus animations in seconds.\&
The value must be finite and greater than zero.\& The default is \fI0.\&25\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBanimation_easing=<easing>\fR
.RS 4
Sets the slide easing curve.\& Available values: \fIlinear\fR,
\fIease_in_expo\fR, \fIease_out_expo\fR, and \fIease_in_out_expo\fR.\& The
default is \fIlinear\fR.\& Other animation modes are not affected.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBactive_color=<color>\fR
.RS 4
Sets the border color for the focused window (format: \fI0xAARRGGBB\fR).\& For
instance, active_color=0xff00ff00 creates a green border.\& Use
\fIdouble(<color>,<color>)\fR to draw independently styled outer and inner
borders.\& Each nested color accepts the complete color syntax described
below.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBinactive_color=<color>\fR
.RS 4
Sets the border color for all windows not in focus.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBbackground_color=<color>\fR
.RS 4
Sets the background fill color for all windows (only \fI0xAARRGGBB\fR arguments supported).\& The background is kept below the application window regardless of the border order.\& Acts as the fallback when a focused-state override is not configured.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBactive_background_color=<color>\fR
.RS 4
Overrides the background fill color for the focused window.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBinactive_background_color=<color>\fR
.RS 4
Overrides the background fill color for unfocused windows.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBbackground_host=<host>\fR
.RS 4
Selects the window that owns background fill and blur.\& \fIauto\fR (the default) uses the border window with \fIorder=below\fR and a target-sized companion window with \fIorder=above\fR.\& \fIborder\fR forces the original single-window implementation with either order; with \fIorder=above\fR, fill and blur are composited above the application.\& \fIcompanion\fR forces the target-sized companion with either order and keeps blur inside the application bounds.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBblur_radius=<float>\fR
.RS 4
Sets the background blur radius.\& Values are clamped to \fI50\fR; \fI0\fR disables blur.\& The blur layer remains below the application window and works with both \fIorder=above\fR and \fIorder=below\fR.\& Below-order borders reuse their existing window; above-order borders use a companion window.\& Blur uses a private macOS API and may affect performance.\& Acts as the fallback when a focused-state override is not configured.\& Hidden and non-visible companion windows are released after a ten-second grace period unless their target becomes visible again first.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBactive_blur_radius=<float>\fR
.RS 4
Overrides the background blur radius for the focused window.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBinactive_blur_radius=<float>\fR
.RS 4
Overrides the background blur radius for unfocused windows.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBwidth=<float>\fR
.RS 4
Determines the width of the border.\& For example, width=5.\&0 creates a border
5.\&0 points wide.\& For double colors, \fIwidth=double(4.\&0,2.\&0)\fR configures the
outer and inner widths respectively.\& A scalar width applies to both layers;
for a single color, only the first double width is used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBdouble_gap=<float>\fR
.RS 4
Sets the transparent gap between double\-border layers.\& The value must be
finite and non\-negative.\& The default is \fI0\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBhidpi=<boolean>\fR
.RS 4
If set to \fIon\fR, the border will be drawn with retina resolution.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBax_focus=<boolean>\fR
.RS 4
If set to \fIon\fR, the (slower) accessibility API is used to resolve the
focused window.\& Enabled automatically if the (parent) process has
accessibility permissions.\& Improves compatibility with other tools
modifying window properties on a system level (e.\&g.\& yabai).\&
.PP
.RE
\fBblacklist=<application_list>\fR
.RS 4
The applications specified here are excluded from being bordered.\& For
example, blacklist="Safari,kitty" excludes Safari and kitty from being
bordered.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBwhitelist=<application_list>\fR
.RS 4
Once this list is populated, only applications listed here are considered
for receiving a border.\& If the whitelist is empty (default) it is inactive.\&
.PP
.RE
If an instance of \fBborders\fR is already running, subsequent invocations will
update the existing process with the new arguments.\&
.PP
If no instance of \fBborders\fR is running and no arguments are supplied, we try
to execute a file at ~/.\&config/borders/bordersrc where a configuration command
could be issued.\&
.PP
.SH NOMENCLATURE
.PP
\fB<float>\fR
.RS 4
A floating point number.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB<boolean>\fR
.RS 4
Either \fIon\fR or \fIoff\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB<style>\fR
.RS 4
Either \fIround\fR, \fIsquare\fR, or \fIuniform\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB<color>\fR
.RS 4
A color formatted as \fI0xAARRGGBB\fR, where \fIA\fR is the alpha channel, \fIR\fR the
red channel, \fIG\fR the green channel and \fIB\fR the blue channel.\& All
constituents take a base 16 number, i.\&e.\& \fI0-F\fR.\&
.PP
The color argument can take the special values: 
.br
	\fIdouble(<color>,<color>)\fR
.br
	\fIgradient(top_left=0xAARRGGBB,bottom_right=0xAARRGGBB)\fR
.br
	\fIgradient(top_right=0xAARRGGBB,bottom_left=0xAARRGGBB)\fR
.br
	\fIglow(0xAARRGGBB)\fR
.br
	\fIglow(gradient(top_left=0xAARRGGBB,bottom_right=0xAARRGGBB))\fR
.br
	\fIglow(gradient(top_right=0xAARRGGBB,bottom_left=0xAARRGGBB))\fR
.br
(You might need to quote these arguments depending on your shell)
The first double color is the outer border and the second is the inner
border.\& Each can independently be a solid color, glow, gradient, or glowing
gradient.\& For example:
.br
	\fIdouble(glow(gradient(top_left=0xffff0000,bottom_right=0xff0000ff)),glow(0xffffffff))\fR
.br
Wrap each nested color independently; \fIglow(double(...))\fR is not supported.\&
.PP
The glow color for a gradient is the alpha-weighted blend of its two
endpoint colors.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB<application_list>\fR
.RS 4
A comma separated list of application names.\& This argument should be
quoted.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH BUGS
.PP
Report them at https://github.\&com/FelixKratz/JankyBorders/issues.\&
.PP
